pe":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The US withholds **15%** on US dividends paid to an Irish UCITS ETF. That is the US–Ireland treaty rate, versus the 30% statutory rate. The fund claims it by filing Form W-8BEN-E with the US payer or custodian.\n\n**Who bears it:** The tax is withheld before dividends reach the fund. It reduces distributions or accumulated NAV, so **investors bear it economically**. It shows up as a drag against a gross-dividend index (MSCI \"net\" indices assume 30%).\n\nKey points:\n- **No further Irish tax** applies at fund level, because Ireland uses a gross roll-up regime. Non-Irish-resident investors with proper declarations face no Irish withholding on distributions.\n- **Investors generally cannot reclaim or credit** the 15%, because it is levied on the fund.\n- This is \"level 1\" withholding. US-domiciled ETFs avoid it but face investor-level US withholding instead.\n- Synthetic (swap-based) UCITS ETFs may reduce the drag under Section 871(m) rules, subject to current regulatory status.","ttft_ms":6685,"type":"result","duration_ms":9236,"uuid":"2763a2cb-5a28-4dd3-a79e-10a847b245f5","ttft_stream_ms":513,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":513,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
